%% The Topic 2 revision sheet — included by KB Part Five and by the RN volume. Must make sense on its own.

## 1. What feminism is

- **Feminism** — the belief that women and men should have **equal rights, dignity and chances**, plus the **action** to achieve it. Two parts: a **theory** (explains why women are disadvantaged) and a **practice** (a movement to change it). Hence the syllabus head "**Feminist Theories and Practice**".
- **Definitions to quote:**
  - **Kamla Bhasin and Nighat Said Khan (1986)** — "an awareness of women's oppression and exploitation in society, at work and within the family, and conscious action by women and men to change this situation." (South Asian; names three sites; includes men.)
  - **bell hooks (2000)** — "a movement to end **sexism** (unfair treatment because of sex), sexist exploitation, and oppression." (Targets sexism, not men.)
  - **Rebecca West (1913)** — called a feminist whenever she refused to be "a doormat or a prostitute".
- **Feminist perspective** — puts **gender at the centre** of analysis (MCQ 2025): always asks "how are women and men affected differently, and why?"
- **The word** — French *féminisme*; **Hubertine Auclert** used "feminist" in **1882**; reached English in the **1890s**. The ideas are much older.
- **Waves** (a metaphor made popular by **Martha Weinman Lear**, 1968) — **first** (c. 1848–1920: vote, property, education; **Seneca Falls 1848**); **second** (1960s–80s: family, work, body, sexuality); **third** (1990s: diversity; **Rebecca Walker 1992**); **fourth** (c. 2012 on: digital; **#MeToo 2017**). Waves follow Western history — a map, not a law. (Full history is Topic 3.)
- **Common ground of all schools** — (1) women are disadvantaged; (2) it is made by society, not nature; (3) the personal and political are linked; (4) women's experience counts as knowledge; (5) change is possible. They **disagree** on **cause**, **site** (where) and **cure**.
- **South Asian and Muslim roots** — **Tarabai Shinde**, *Stri Purush Tulana* (1882); **Sayyid Mumtaz Ali**, *Huquq-un-Niswan* (Lahore, 1898, rights from the Qur'an); **Qasim Amin**, *The Liberation of Women* (Egypt, 1899); **Begum Rokeya**, *Sultana's Dream* (1905); **APWA** (1949, Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan); **WAF** (1981). **Quaid-e-Azam, Aligarh 1944:** "No nation can rise to the height of glory unless your women are side by side with you."
- **Myths** — not man-hating (targets sexism); not one ideology (a family of theories); not only Western; not always anti-religion (Muslim feminism); not "women becoming men"; not outdated (Pakistan **143rd of 145**, Global Gender Gap Report 2026; women's labour-force participation **24.4%**, LFS 2024–25).
- **The four questions that separate the schools** — **cause** (why?), **site** (law, family, economy, mind, language?), **remedy** (reform or transform?), **method** (laws, protest, revolution, therapy, new language?).

## 2. Liberal feminism

- **Definition** — women are as **rational** as men; their disadvantage comes from **unequal laws, customs and opportunities**; remedy = **reform** (equal rights, education, work, political access) **within** the existing system. MCQ 2023: "men and women should be treated equally" = **liberal feminism**.
- **Roots** — **liberalism** of the Enlightenment: the **individual**, **reason**, **natural rights**, equal citizenship.
- **First wave:**
  - **Mary Wollstonecraft**, *A Vindication of the Rights of Woman* (**1792**) — answered **Rousseau**'s *Émile* (1762), which said girls should be educated to please men; women seem weak because denied a **rational education**.
  - **Olympe de Gouges**, *Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen* (1791); executed 1793.
  - **Seneca Falls** (1848) — **Stanton** and **Mott**'s **Declaration of Sentiments**: "all men and women are created equal".
  - **J. S. Mill**, *The Subjection of Women* (**1869**), with **Harriet Taylor Mill** (*The Enfranchisement of Women*, 1851) — legal subordination of women is wrong and blocks progress; women's "nature" is "an eminently artificial thing"; 1867 motion to replace "man" with "person".
  - Vote: New Zealand 1893; USA 1920; Britain 1918 (partial), 1928 (equal).
- **Second wave:**
  - **Betty Friedan**, *The Feminine Mystique* (**1963**) — "**the problem that has no name**": unhappiness of educated housewives; the "mystique" = belief that women are fulfilled only as wives and mothers. Co-founded **NOW** (1966).
  - US **Equal Pay Act** (1963); **Title VII, Civil Rights Act** (1964, no sex discrimination in jobs); **CEDAW** (UN women's-rights treaty, 1979; Pakistan joined **1996**).
- **Later** — **Susan Moller Okin** (1989): the **family** must be just; **Martha Nussbaum** (2000): **capabilities approach** (real freedom to be and do — health, education, safety, work); **Zillah Eisenstein** (1981).
- **Strengths** — won the vote, education, equal-pay and anti-discrimination laws; persuasive rights language; practical, works through courts and parliaments; fits CEDAW and **SDG 5**.
- **Criticisms** — ignores **structures** and unpaid care; keeps the state out of the **private sphere** (family); equality = **sameness** with a **male norm**; **middle-class bias** (hooks on Friedan); **individualism**.
- **Pakistan** — **Article 25** (equality; no sex discrimination; special provision for women allowed), **Article 27** (no sex discrimination in public service), **Article 34** (full participation of women); laws of 2006 (Protection of Women), 2010 (workplace harassment), 2016 (honour killing), 2021 (anti-rape), **ICT Child Marriage Restraint Act 2025** (age 18); **60 reserved seats**; **NCSW** (2000); **Malala** (Nobel 2014). **Limit:** weak enforcement, poverty and custom.

## 3. Radical feminism

- **Definition** — "radical" from Latin ***radix*** = **root**. **Patriarchy** (male power over women) is the **oldest, deepest** oppression, present everywhere — especially in **family, sexuality, body and violence**. Remedy: **transform** society at the root. MCQ 2019: **replacing patriarchy** = a **radical** idea.
- **Origin** — USA, **late 1960s**; women in civil-rights and anti-war "**New Left**" movements were sidelined by male comrades. **New York Radical Women** (1967; Miss America protest 1968 — "freedom trash can", no bras actually burned); **Redstockings** (1969): "Women are an oppressed class."
- **"The personal is political"** — **Carol Hanisch** (written 1969, published 1970): private problems (housework, sex, marriage) are **political** — shared, and caused by power. **Consciousness-raising** = small groups sharing experiences to reach political awareness.
- **Thinkers:**
  - **Kate Millett**, *Sexual Politics* (**1970**) — **sexual politics** = power relations between the sexes; made **patriarchy** the key concept; found it in family, religion, literature, force. MCQs 2016, 2023: Millett = **radical feminist**.
  - **Shulamith Firestone**, *The Dialectic of Sex* (**1970**, MCQ 2016) — root in **biology/reproduction**; women a "**sex class**"; seize control of reproduction; imagined artificial reproduction.
  - **Robin Morgan**, *Sisterhood Is Powerful* (1970); **Susan Brownmiller**, *Against Our Will* (1975) — **rape** keeps all women in fear; **Mary Daly**, *Gyn/Ecology* (1978); **Adrienne Rich**, *Of Woman Born* (1976), "**Compulsory Heterosexuality**" (1980); **Catharine MacKinnon** (1979) — **sexual harassment = sex discrimination**; with **Andrea Dworkin**, anti-pornography campaign (1983).
- **Two kinds** (Rosemarie Tong) — **radical-libertarian** (femininity is a trap; aim androgyny; Firestone) vs **radical-cultural** (female values are better; celebrate the female; Daly, Rich).
- **Achievements** — **rape crisis centres**; **women's shelters** (first modern one, Chiswick, London, 1971); domestic violence made a **public crime**; **sexual harassment** law; **marital rape** recognised; **bodily autonomy** (right to decide about one's own body).
- **Criticisms** — treats all women as one (ignores **race, class** — hooks, Lorde); **biological determinism** (Firestone); **anti-men** image; patriarchy too universal to explain change; divisive "**sex wars**" (1980s splits over pornography); backlash in conservative societies.
- **Pakistan** — **WAF** (1981) against the **Hudood Ordinances** (1979, rape victims at risk of adultery charges); laws on harassment (2010), **acid** (2011), **honour killing** (2016), **rape** (2021); **Aurat March** (from 8 March **2018**) slogan ***Mera Jism Meri Marzi*** ("My body, my choice") = bodily autonomy; controversy 2019–20. **Line:** radical analysis of violence is powerful; argue it in constitutional and Islamic language.

## 4. Marxist and socialist feminism

- **Marxist terms** — **capitalism** (few own the means of production; most sell labour); **class** (owners/bourgeoisie vs workers/proletariat); **exploitation** (workers paid less than the value they produce — profit); **base/superstructure** (economy shapes law, family, ideas); **reserve army of labour** (workers hired in booms, dropped in slumps).
- **Marxist feminism** — oppression rooted in **capitalism and private property**; women exploited as **unpaid domestic workers** and as a cheap **reserve army**; gender oppression = form of **class** oppression; ends when capitalism ends.
- **Classical:**
  - **Engels**, *The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State* (**1884**) — private property → men want heirs → **monogamy imposed on women** → "**world historical defeat of the female sex**"; based on **Lewis Henry Morgan**, *Ancient Society* (1877). Cure: end private property; women into paid work.
  - **August Bebel**, *Woman and Socialism* (1879); **Clara Zetkin** — proposed **International Women's Day**, Copenhagen **1910** (first held 1911; UN from **1975**, 8 March); **Alexandra Kollontai** — Soviet communal kitchens and childcare.
- **Domestic-labour debate** — **Margaret Benston** (1969): housework is real production; **Dalla Costa and Selma James** (1972): housework produces **the worker**; **Wages for Housework** (1972); **Silvia Federici** (1975): "They say it is love. We say it is unwaged work." **Social reproduction** = unpaid work that keeps people alive and able to work (cooking, cleaning, childcare, care of sick and old) — **Lise Vogel** (1983), **Tithi Bhattacharya** (2017).
- **Socialist feminism** — **capitalism and patriarchy together**; studies paid and unpaid work, class and gender; change economy **and** family.
  - **Juliet Mitchell** — "Women: The Longest Revolution" (1966), *Woman's Estate* (**1971**): **four structures** — production, reproduction, sexuality, socialisation of children.
  - **Heidi Hartmann**, "**The Unhappy Marriage of Marxism and Feminism**" (**1979**) — Marxism "**sex-blind**"; **dual systems** (capitalism + patriarchy); male unions' **family wage** pushed women home.
  - **Zillah Eisenstein** (1979) — "**capitalist patriarchy**"; **Iris Marion Young** (1980) — **unified systems**; **Maria Mies** (1986) — "**housewifisation**" (women's work everywhere treated as secondary and cheap); **Sylvia Walby** (1990) — **six structures** of patriarchy (paid work, household production, culture, sexuality, violence, state).
- **Stance** — materialism; two exploitations (paid and unpaid); **double burden / "second shift"** (**Hochschild**, 1989); family serves capital; women as reserve army; liberation needs **economic independence and socialised care** (public childcare, paid leave).
- **Criticisms** — sex-blind; reduces gender to economics; underplays violence and sexuality; socialist states left women a **double burden**; wages for housework may fix women at home; dual systems hard to link.
- **Pakistan** — most employed women in **agriculture**, much as **unpaid family helpers**; **home-based workers** (Sindh Act **2018**, first); **Lady Health Worker Programme** (**1994**; Supreme Court regularisation order 2012); **Sindhiani Tehreek** (from 1982, rural Sindhi women); **Okara** tenants' movement (from 2000, women's groups); **BISP** (2008, cash to women); **Women Democratic Front** (2018).

## 5. Psychoanalytic feminism

- **Definition** — oppression rooted in the **psyche** (mind): the **unconscious** (hidden desires and fears) and **early childhood** in the family produce "masculine" and "feminine" personalities; change needs new **childrearing** and new **cultural meanings**.
- **Freud** — **Oedipus complex** (age 3–5): boy desires mother, fears **castration**, identifies with father → strong **superego** (conscience); girl discovers "lack", **penis envy**, turns to father, wants a baby (son) → **weaker superego** (1925); "**Anatomy is destiny**" (1924); "Femininity" (1933).
- **The attack** — **Karen Horney**, "The Flight from Womanhood" (**1926**): girls envy men's **social power**, not the penis; **womb envy** — men envy women's power to give birth and compensate by achievement and domination; **Clara Thompson** (1940s): envy of cultural privilege; **Beauvoir** (1949): body's meaning is social; **Friedan** (1963), **Millett** (1970) blamed Freud for keeping women home.
- **The re-reading** — **Juliet Mitchell**, *Psychoanalysis and Feminism* (**1974**): psychoanalysis is "not a recommendation *for* a patriarchal society, but an analysis *of* one."
- **Object-relations (mothering)** — **Dorothy Dinnerstein**, *The Mermaid and the Minotaur* (**1976**): women alone care for infants → adults fear female power → control women; remedy: **shared parenting**. **Nancy Chodorow**, *The Reproduction of Mothering* (**1978**): girls stay close to mother → **relational self**, wish to care; boys separate → masculinity as "not-mother", **devalue the feminine**; mothering **reproduces** gender; remedy: **dual parenting**. **Carol Gilligan**, *In a Different Voice* (1982): **ethic of care** (relationships) vs **Kohlberg**'s **ethic of justice** (rules, rights).
- **Lacan and the French** — **Lacan**: entry into language (**symbolic order**) under the "**Law of the Father**"; **phallus** = symbol of power, not organ. **Irigaray** (*Speculum*, 1974; *This Sex Which Is Not One*, 1977) — woman only a mirror for man; **Kristeva** (1974) — maternal **semiotic** (rhythm, feeling) disturbs paternal **symbolic**; **Cixous**, "The Laugh of the Medusa" (**1975**) — **écriture féminine** ("feminine writing" from the body; MCQ 2024).
- **How it explains subordination** — men's fear and envy; masculinity built by rejecting the mother; femininity as self-sacrifice; mothering repeats; culture male-centred; oppression **internalised** (women accept or defend it).
- **Correct the 2021 wording** — men's need to dominate is **deep and unconscious but produced, not "inherent"** — so it can change.
- **Criticisms** — untestable; assumes Western **nuclear family**; ignores money, law, violence; **mother-blaming**; heteronormative (assumes mother–father family); abstract.
- **Pakistan** — **son preference**; boys moved to male spaces and taught not to cry; mother–son bond and mother-in-law power (**Kandiyoti's patriarchal bargain**); internalised limits on mobility; value of **fathers in childcare** and paternity leave.

## 6. Men's feminism

- **Definition** — men's support for gender equality (**pro-feminism**) and analysis of how **patriarchy shapes and harms men**; aims to change masculinity and male-run institutions.
- **History** — **Condorcet** (1790); **Raja Ram Mohan Roy** (against *sati*, banned 1829); **Frederick Douglass** (only African-American signer at Seneca Falls, 1848); **Vidyasagar** (Widows' Remarriage Act 1856); **Mill** (1869); **Mumtaz Ali** (1898); **Qasim Amin** (1899); **Sheikh Abdullah "Papa Mian"** (girls' school, Aligarh, 1906); **Jinnah** (1944).
- **Thinkers** — **R. W. Connell**, *Masculinities* (1995): **hegemonic masculinity** (the honoured, dominant ideal of manhood) and **patriarchal dividend** (advantage all men gain); **Michael Kimmel** (1996, 2008): manhood proved before other men; **Michael Messner** (1997): men's movements mapped by privilege, costs, differences; **bell hooks**, *The Will to Change* (2004): patriarchy wounds men emotionally; **Michael Flood**: violence prevention with men.
- **Men's movements** — **pro-feminist** (NOMAS); **men's liberation** (1970s, costs of male role); **mythopoetic** (**Robert Bly**, *Iron John*, 1990); **men's rights** (anti-feminist: custody, "false accusations").
- **Campaigns** — **Montreal massacre** (6 Dec 1989, 14 women killed) → **White Ribbon** (Canada, **1991**; pledge never to commit, excuse or stay silent about violence against women); **ICPD Cairo 1994** (male responsibility); **Beijing 1995**; **MenEngage** (2004); **HeForShe** (UN Women, **20 Sept 2014**, Emma Watson).
- **Costs of patriarchy for men** — shorter lives; higher suicide; most homicide victims; provider burden (in Pakistan: dowries, migration, debt); distant fatherhood.
- **Debate: can men be feminists?** — yes (hooks: against sexism, not men); yes as **allies, not leaders**; caution (image-seeking, diverted funds); no (few radicals). **Accepted view:** pro-feminist allies, accountable to women.
- **Pakistan** — decisions on girls' school, marriage, work, inheritance made by fathers, brothers, husbands, elders → engage them; **Rozan** (Islamabad; boys, men, police); **Ziauddin Yousafzai** (*Let Her Fly*, 2018); male scholars on inheritance and consent.
- **Criticisms** — centres men; privilege remains; slides into men's rights; men dominate spaces; weak evidence of lasting behaviour change.

## 7. Postmodern and post-structural feminism

- **Definition** — **no universal "woman"**; no single cause or truth; identities are **constructed in language and discourse**, multiple and unstable; task: **deconstruct** binaries (man/woman, reason/emotion) and value **plurality and difference**.
- **MCQ key** — post-structural feminism emphasises "**the contingent and discursive nature of identities**" (2018, 2023). **Contingent** = not fixed, could be otherwise. **Discursive** = made by **discourse** (how society talks and thinks about something, and who may speak).
- **Roots** — **Lyotard**, *The Postmodern Condition* (**1979**): "**incredulity toward metanarratives**" (disbelief in grand stories like progress or class revolution); **Derrida** (1967): **deconstruction** of ranked opposites; **Foucault** (1975, 1976): **power/knowledge**, discourses define "normal". **Structuralism** = fixed structures of meaning; **post-structuralism** = meaning never fixed.
- **Core ideas** — anti-essentialism; difference among women; language constructs reality; suspicion of grand theories; value of **local and personal knowledge** (MCQ 2025, women's personal experiences → postmodern, suggested key).
- **Thinkers** — **Butler**, *Gender Trouble* (**1990**): gender **performative** (repeated acts create it); *Bodies That Matter* (1993); **Cixous, Irigaray, Kristeva** (French feminism, écriture féminine) — explains MCQ 2019: **gender equality through literature** = **postmodern feminism**; **Jane Flax** (1987); **Joan Scott** (1986; 1988, equality vs difference a false binary); **Donna Haraway**, "A Cyborg Manifesto" (1985); **Chris Weedon** (1987); **Fraser and Nicholson** (1988, middle way).
- **Modern vs postmodern** — one "women" vs many womanhoods; one main cause vs many forms of power; universal truths vs local truths; one path vs plural struggles.
- **Strengths** — includes excluded women; attention to language and media; no false universals; change always possible.
- **Criticisms** — **Seyla Benhabib** (1995): "**death of the subject**" leaves no agent; **political paralysis** (who does feminism speak for?); **relativism** (can't condemn honour killing universally); **jargon** — **Nussbaum**, "The Professor of Parody" (1999); neglects material life.
- **Answer to the critics** — **Spivak's strategic essentialism** = **temporary strategic unity around an identity** for political goals (MCQ 2026).
- **Pakistan** — no single "Pakistani woman" (banker, Thari peasant, Hazara widow, *khawaja sira*); **honour** (*ghairat*) **as discourse**; media images (fairness creams, dramas); poets **Kishwar Naheed** ("We Sinful Women") and **Fahmida Riaz**.

## 8. Beyond the syllabus schools

- **Black feminism** — race, class and gender together (MCQ 2025: "**all of these**"). **Sojourner Truth**, "Ain't I a Woman?" (Akron, Ohio, **1851**; famous version published 1863); **Combahee River Collective** (**1977**): **interlocking**, simultaneous oppressions; **bell hooks**, *Ain't I a Woman* (1981), *From Margin to Center* (1984); **Audre Lorde**, "The Master's Tools…" (1979); **Alice Walker**, **womanism** (1983).
- **Intersectionality** — **Kimberlé Crenshaw** (**1989**; "Mapping the Margins" 1991): identities (gender, race, class, religion, ethnicity, caste, disability, age, sexuality) **overlap and interact**, creating **unique** disadvantage; not "added up" but **multiplied**. Origin: **gender and race** (MCQ 2024). Case: ***DeGraffenreid v. General Motors*** (1976) — Black women fell between race and sex claims. Image: **traffic intersection**. Tool: ask "**which women?**"
- **Matrix of domination** — **Patricia Hill Collins**, *Black Feminist Thought* (**1990**): how intersecting oppressions are organised across society (MCQ 2026); "**outsider within**".
- **Intersectionality in Pakistan** — rural women (distance, literacy gap; female literacy about 53%, Census 2023); minority women (reported **forced conversions and marriages** in Sindh and Punjab); **Hazara** women; women with disabilities; **khawaja siras**; class (domestic workers of professional women).
- **Standpoint theory** — **knowledge is socially situated** (MCQ 2026): the oppressed see what the powerful miss. **Nancy Hartsock** (1983); **Dorothy Smith** (1987); **Sandra Harding** (1986, "**strong objectivity**"); **Collins** (1990). Roots: Hegel, Marx.
- **Postcolonial feminism** — **Chandra Talpade Mohanty**, "**Under Western Eyes**" (**1984**/1986): Western feminism creates one victimised "**Third World Woman**"; **Gayatri Spivak**, "**Can the Subaltern Speak?**" (**1988**): "White men are saving brown women from brown men" (on *sati*); **subaltern** = voiceless person at the bottom; **Lila Abu-Lughod**, "Do Muslim Women Really Need Saving?" (2002); **Uma Narayan** (1997). Pakistan use: answers "Western agenda" charge, but warns against defending every custom.
- **Ecofeminism** — domination of **women** and of **nature** linked; coined by **Françoise d'Eaubonne** (**1974**); **Carolyn Merchant**, *The Death of Nature* (1980); **Vandana Shiva**, *Staying Alive* (1988); Mies and Shiva, *Ecofeminism* (1993); **Chipko** (1973); **Wangari Maathai**, Green Belt (1977; Nobel 2004). **Critique — Bina Agarwal** (1992), **feminist environmentalism**: women's link to nature comes from **material work** (water, fuel, fodder), not biology. Pakistan: **2022 floods** (about 33 million affected), Thar, Cholistan.
- **Muslim (Islamic) feminism** — gender justice by **re-reading Qur'an and Sunnah**, separating divine message from patriarchal **tafsir** (interpretation) and custom; values of **adl** (justice), equal faith, dignity. Term spread in 1990s (*Zanan*, Iran, 1992; **Margot Badran**). **Fatima Mernissi** (*Beyond the Veil* 1975; *The Veil and the Male Elite* 1987 — questioned hadith chains); **Amina Wadud**, *Qur'an and Woman* (**1992**); **Leila Ahmed**, *Women and Gender in Islam* (1992); **Riffat Hassan** (Pakistani-American; woman not created from a rib); **Asma Barlas**, *"Believing Women" in Islam* (2002; Qur'an anti-patriarchal). **Sisters in Islam** (Malaysia, 1988); **Musawah** (2009). Debate: bridge (legitimacy; inheritance, *mahr* = marriage gift to the bride, *khula* = wife-initiated divorce, consent) vs contradiction (**Haideh Moghissi** 1999; **Afiya Zia**, *Faith and Feminism in Pakistan* 2018) vs "Western agenda" (conservatives); **Saba Mahmood**, *Politics of Piety* (2005): pious women have agency.
- **More terms** — **patriarchal bargain** (**Deniz Kandiyoti**, "Bargaining with Patriarchy", **1988**): women's strategies to gain security within patriarchy; in "**classic patriarchy**" (North Africa–Middle East–South Asia) the young bride later gains power as mother of sons and mother-in-law. **Kyriarchy** (**Elisabeth Schüssler Fiorenza**, *But She Said*, **1992**; Greek *kyrios* = lord): **interlocking structures of domination**. **Gynocriticism** (**Elaine Showalter**, **1979**; *A Literature of Their Own* 1977): study of women writers. **Écriture féminine** (**Cixous**, 1975).

## 9. The schools compared

- **Master line for each** — **Liberal:** unequal laws → reform. **Radical:** patriarchy → transform at root. **Marxist:** capitalism → end capitalism. **Socialist:** capitalism + patriarchy → change both. **Psychoanalytic:** unconscious/childhood → shared parenting. **Men's:** patriarchy harms men too → men as allies. **Postmodern:** fixed categories/discourse → deconstruct. **Intersectional:** overlapping systems → target the overlaps. **Postcolonial:** Western dominance → local feminisms. **Eco:** domination of women and nature → care-based economy. **Muslim:** patriarchal interpretation → re-read sources.
- **Radical vs socialist (2016)** — enemy: **patriarchy** vs **capitalist patriarchy**; unit: **sex** vs **sex and class**; would ending capitalism free women? **no** vs **not alone**; men: beneficiaries vs working-class men also exploited, can be allies; concepts: sexual politics, sex class, personal is political vs dual systems, family wage, double burden, social reproduction; campaigns: shelters, bodily autonomy vs equal pay, childcare, wages for housework; weakness: ignores class/race vs neglects violence/sexuality. **Agree:** family is a site of oppression; personal is political; want deep change.
- **Liberal vs radical** — reform vs transform; public vs private and public; equality as same chances vs ending male power.
- **Marxist vs socialist** — capitalism vs capitalism **and** patriarchy; Engels (1884) vs Hartmann (1979).

## 10. Which feminism suits Pakistan (2018)

- **Trap** — "type(s)": argue for **more than one**; do not write Topic 3's movement history.
- **Context** — Global Gender Gap 2026 **143/145**, **59.5%** (economic **144th**, education **130th**, political **107th**); LFPR **24.4%** (2024–25); literacy ≈ **68% men, 53% women** (2023); MMR **186** per 100,000 live births (2019); only **11** women won general NA seats (2024); **60** reserved seats.
- **The plural model** —
  1. **Liberal** → legal and political status (Articles 25, 27, 34; laws; quotas; education). Limit: enforcement.
  2. **Islamic feminism** → socio-cultural legitimacy (inheritance, *mahr*, *khula*, consent, education argued from faith).
  3. **Socialist** → economic status (childcare, safe transport, recognition of unpaid work, rights of home-based and domestic workers, BISP).
  4. **Radical insight** → safety and bodily integrity (laws on acid 2011, honour 2016, rape 2021).
  5. **Men's feminism** → engage gatekeepers (fathers, husbands, clerics, police).
  6. **Intersectionality** → reach rural, poor, minority and disabled women and *khawaja siras* first.
- **Least fit, still useful** — pure radical (backlash; keep the analysis); pure Marxist (no revolution coming; keep unpaid-work insight); postmodern (abstract; warns against one "Pakistani woman"); psychoanalytic (no quick policy; shared parenting).
- **Conclusion line** — a **plural, home-grown feminism**: liberal in law, Islamic in justification, socialist in economics, radical against violence, inclusive of men, intersectional in reach — as in Pakistan's own successful reforms (**MFLO 1961** to the **2016** honour-killing law).

## 11. Today's debates in one line each

- **Western import?** — word French, struggle universal, local roots (Shinde, Mumtaz Ali, Rokeya, Quaid 1944).
- **Islamic feminism** — bridge vs contradiction; lasting reforms combined Constitution and Islam.
- **Aurat March slogans** — radical visibility vs liberal persuasion; substance (freedom from violence) widely supported; Article 14 (dignity).
- **Which women?** — intersectional targeting.
- **Care economy** — COVID-19; **SDG 5.4** (recognise and value unpaid care); time-use surveys; day-care; leave.
- **Digital fourth wave** — **#MeToo** (Tarana Burke 2006; global 2017; Pakistan 2018); online abuse; **PECA 2016**.
- **Climate** — 2022 floods; ecofeminism vs feminist environmentalism (Agarwal).
- **Men** — allies (White Ribbon, HeForShe, Rozan) vs men's-rights backlash; caring manhood.
